        "created_at": "2025-11-06T08:08:33.457Z",
        "cooked": "<p>You are setting (assigning) the value of <code>var_sum</code> to 8959 in the first line.</p>\n<p>When you use</p>\n<pre data-code-wrap=\"bash\"><code class=\"lang-bash\">echo ${var_sum:+9000}\n</code></pre>\n<p>you get 9000 as output.</p>\n<p>This uses the <strong><code>${parameter:+word}</code></strong> expansion form.<br>\nIf <em>parameter</em> is <strong>set and not null</strong>, substitute <strong><code>word</code></strong>. Otherwise, substitute <strong>nothing</strong> (null).</p>\n<p>So in you case <code>var_sum</code> is already set and has a non-empty value (<code>8959</code>), therefore Bash replaces the whole expression with <code>9000</code>.</p>\n<p>It does not change the value of <code>var_sum</code> — it only substitutes <strong>the word after <code>+</code></strong>. So, it doesn’t modify the value of the variable.</p>\n<p>This <code>${parameter:+word}</code> is just a check to check a variable is set or not.</p>",
